pillow library needed for testGen.py

The testbench uses a runtime plusarg mechanism to pass values into the simulation without modifying the source code.
A plusarg is an argument given when the simulator is launched, usually in the form +NAME=value.
This is useful when the same testbench needs to be reused with different settings across multiple simulation runs.

In Verilog, this is commonly done with $value$plusargs.
Here, the $value part means that the simulator not only checks for the existence of a runtime argument, but also extracts the value associated with it.
For example, if the simulator is launched with +EXPECTED_LABEL=7, $value$plusargs can parse the 7 and store it in a variable.
This is different from $test$plusargs, which only checks whether a plusarg is present and does not read any value from it.

In this testbench, the plusarg mechanism is used to set the expected classification label at runtime.

##############################
integer expected_label_int;
reg[3:0] EXPECTED_LABEL;

initial begin
	expected_label_int =0;
	if ($value$plusargs("EXPECTED_LABEL=%d",expected_label_int)) EXPECTED_LABEL =expected_label_int[3:0]; 
	else EXPECTED_LABEL = 4'd0;
    $display("EXPECTED_LABEL =%0d",EXPECTED_LABEL);
end
##############################

n this code, expected_label_int is a temporary integer variable used to receive the value read from the runtime plusarg.
EXPECTED_LABEL is the 4-bit signal that is actually used inside the testbench.

At the start of simulation, the code first assigns a default value of 0.
Then it calls $value$plusargs("EXPECTED_LABEL=%d", expected_label_int).
This tells the simulator to look for a runtime argument matching the pattern +EXPECTED_LABEL=<decimal number>.
If such an argument is found, the decimal value is parsed and written into expected_label_int, and then its lower 4 bits are copied into EXPECTED_LABEL.
If the argument is not provided, the testbench falls back to 4'd0.

This makes it possible to change the expected label for each simulation run without editing the testbench source or recompiling the design.